=== p2-windows · W7 · page:system/Roadmap (WinUI 3 / Fluent) ===
Parity unit : page:system/Roadmap   kind=page   web route=/roadmap
Web source  : web/src/features/system/pages/RoadmapPage.tsx
Output      : apps/windows/TeslaSync.App/feature-views/RoadmapPage/{RoadmapPage.Model.cs, RoadmapPage.ViewModel.cs, RoadmapPage.cs}
ADR refs    : ADR-002, ADR-004, ADR-005, ADR-006, ADR-011, ADR-013, ADR-014, ADR-015
Runner      : .NET 10.0.300 SDK present (dotnet --version 10.0.300) — NOT blocked.

=== APPROACH ===
- The repository's WinUI app is code-first C# (no .xaml page files); every feature surface lives under
  apps/windows/TeslaSync.App/feature-views/<Name>/ as <Name>.Model.cs (pure, Microsoft.UI-free projection,
  unit-tested headlessly), an optional <Name>.Source.cs (the generated-client feed) and <Name>.ViewModel.cs
  (INotifyPropertyChanged state holder), plus <Name>.cs (the Fluent view), exactly as the sibling W7 HelpPage /
  ChatbotPage / StatusApiDocsPage do. The generated prompt's idealized
  "TeslaSync/Features/System/RoadmapPage.xaml + RoadmapPageViewModel.cs" is mapped onto these real conventions.
  The namespace is TeslaSync.App.FeatureViews.SystemOps (NOT ...System — a `.System` sub-namespace shadows the
  global System namespace and breaks unqualified System.* references; SystemOps also aligns with
  RouteGroup.SystemOps, the manifest's system/ group).
- Data binding (ADR-004): the manifest declares NO API data sources — the web page renders from local state only
  (it binds just useTranslation + usePageTitle and maps the static roadmapItems catalog). So there is no
  generated-client feed and no <Name>.Source.cs; RoadmapPageViewModel projects the static RoadmapCatalog through
  RoadmapProjection with zero HTTP. LoadAsync/RefreshAsync honour the page lifecycle (OnLoaded → LoadAsync) by
  re-projecting synchronously (no fabricated network call).
- Two data states (manifest: empty · success): the web page has no fetch/loading/error branch, so the honest
  native union is RoadmapState.Success (the catalog yielded entries → progress bar + the per-phase card sections)
  plus a defensive RoadmapState.Empty branch (a null/empty catalog → the progress bar with all-zero counts + a
  friendly TsEmptyState, never a blank region, ADR-011). The phase progress bar is always visible.
- Panels (ADR-005): GlassPanel1 = the always-visible phase progress bar (web <GlassPanel className="p-4">) — a
  TsGlassPanel hosting the four phase chips (a coloured dot + the localized phase label + a TsBadge count),
  separated by hairline rules, in a horizontally-scrollable row (web overflow-x-auto). GlassPanel2 = the roadmap
  card — one TsGlassPanel per roadmapItems entry (web <GlassPanel className="p-5">), grouped under its release
  phase and laid out in a responsive 1/2-column grid (web grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2; single-card phases stay
  one column; breakpoint 1024) that recomputes on SizeChanged. Each card carries an accent icon tile, the
  SemiBold title + muted one-line description, a phase chip (TsBadge keyed by the web phaseConfig.variant) and the
  capability bullet list whose leading glyph is keyed by phase (done → CheckCircle, current → Zap, otherwise
  Clock). No hardcoded colours/typography — DisplayTokens (TsColor*/TsChart*/TsRadius*) drive
  light/dark/high-contrast from W1. The web per-phase colours (#10B981 / #00F0FF / #A855F7 / #F59E0B) resolve to
  the themed tokens TsChartBatteryBrush / TsColorInfoBrush / TsChartPowerBrush / TsChartEnergyBrush — exact-hue
  matches, never an ad-hoc hex.
- Navigation entry: the web /roadmap route is already mapped by the shell — RouteTable.cs L195
  Page("Roadmap","roadmap",RouteGroup.SystemOps,"Roadmap","\uE8A7") (a visible nav destination, matching the web's
  routed status). This change only registers the page factory in ShellWindow.xaml.cs
  (RoadmapRegistration.RouteName → new FeatureViews.SystemOps.RoadmapPage()); the page renders only its static
  catalog and navigates nowhere, so no NavigationRequested wiring is needed and no new RouteTable row is added
  (NavigationTests stays green — no route-table change).
- SI units N/A (this surface has no unit-bearing fields — only static roadmap copy); no charts, no maps; no
  SSE/live source and therefore no >2-min staleness pill (ADR-013 N/A).
- i18n (ADR-014): every visible chrome literal resolves through the ILocalizer facade with the web key names +
  English defaults. roadmap.title + roadmap.subtitle are already present verbatim (web key names) in
  Strings/{en,ar,he}/Resources.resw as translation.roadmap.* with the web English values; no catalog edit was
  needed. The phase labels mirror the web exactly — the web calls t('roadmap.phase.${phase}', fallback) where the
  roadmap.phase.* keys are NOT defined in the web i18n catalog (web en.json roadmap = { title, subtitle } only),
  so the web renders the English fallbacks; the native port routes them through
  localizer.GetString("roadmap.phase.*", "Completed"/"In Progress"/"Up Next"/"Future") which returns the same
  fallbacks — no resw edit, no divergence. The roadmap card titles/descriptions/features are static content data
  (the web hardcodes them in roadmapItems, NOT via i18n), ported verbatim. The defensive empty message routes
  through localizer.GetString("roadmap.empty", …) (fallback only). RecordingLocalizer asserts every key flows
  through the facade.
- a11y (ADR-015): GlassPanel1 carries AutomationProperties.Name; each phase chip exposes a composed Narrator name
  ("<label>: <count>"); the card region is named; each phase section heading carries HeadingLevel=Level2; each
  card's TsGlassPanel carries its composed Narrator name (title + description); decorative icon tiles / dots /
  bullets / chips are AccessibilityView.Raw; the page exposes a Group automation peer; reduce-motion is honoured
  via TsFadeIn; DisplayTokens drive light/dark + high-contrast.

